<o:Entity Id="o922">
<a:ObjectID>DB0C0D28-0BD3-4808-AA36-2F685DEC80C9</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>Reservoir</a:Name>
<a:Code>RSRVR</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1775817984</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>Elton.Manoku</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1775818154</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>Elton.Manoku</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>A subsurface rock formation containing an individual and separate natural accumulation of moveable petroleum/hydrocarbons that is confined by impermeable rocks/formations and is characterized by a single-pressure system.
Notes:
o Every field has at least one reservoir and that a reservoir is unique to a field; if two fields have the same name for a reservoir (e.g. Rotliegend) then this should actually be two separate reservoirs in the database

o The definition is a guide, not as an absolute rule. Most of the terms in the definition are subject to interpretation and the subsurface remains inherently uncertain, so the priority must be to capture data in a meaningful way that allows us to continue to properly manages our assets.

o Petroleum is defined as a naturally occurring mixture consisting of hydrocarbons in the gaseous, liquid, or solid phase. Petroleum may also contain non-hydrocarbon compounds, common examples of which are carbon dioxide, nitrogen, hydrogen sulfide, and sulfur. In rare cases, non-hydrocarbon content could be greater than 50%.</a:Comment>
