<o:Entity Id="o934">
<a:ObjectID>9A7E9A4E-24BC-4748-A786-53AD69472145</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>Property Model</a:Name>
<a:Code>PRPRTY_MDL</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1775817984</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>Elton.Manoku</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1775818154</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>Elton.Manoku</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>A 3D property model improves the understanding of hydrocarbon distribution for volumetric analysis of flow in the carbonate reservoirs. There are two main sources of rock property data: wireline logs and cores. In property modelling, the results of a petrophysical interpretation based on indirect measurement of physical responses of rock and fluid are used. The rock and fluid properties that are measured include porosity, water saturation, permeability, poro-perm relationship, capillary pressure, and wettability. At the simplest, to model, only porosity, water saturation and permeability are required, but complications arise with understanding the relationships between these properties. Rock typing is a process that combines geological and petrophysical characterization at the core scale that can be applied at the wireline log and even seismic scale. The range of uncertainty on the property models is small compared with the larger scale issues of depth conversion and facies modelling.
</a:Comment>
<a:Description>{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\nouicompat\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Futura Medium;}{\f1\fnil Futura Medium;}{\f2\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}
{\colortbl ;\red89\green89\blue89;}
{\*\generator Riched20 10.0.17763}\viewkind4\uc1 
\pard\cf1\kerning24\f0\fs16 A 3D property model improves the understanding of hydrocarbon distribution for volumetric analysis of flow in the carbonate reservoirs. There are two main sources of rock property data: wireline logs and cores. In property modelling, the results of a petrophysical interpretation based on indirect measurement of physical responses of rock and fluid are used. The rock and fluid properties that are measured include porosity, water saturation, permeability, poro\f1\u8208?\f0 perm relationship, capillary pressure, and wettability. At the simplest, to model, only porosity, water saturation and permeability are required, but complications arise with understanding the relationships between these properties. Rock typing is a process that combines geological and petrophysical characterization at the core scale that can be applied at the wireline log and even seismic scale. The range of uncertainty on the property models is small compared with the larger scale issues of depth conversion and facies modelling.\par

\pard\cf0\kerning0\f2\fs20\par
}
</a:Description>
