<o:Entity Id="o1254">
<a:ObjectID>EE151D37-CF14-4D52-BE23-4CBC891A8017</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>Production Forecast</a:Name>
<a:Code>PRDCTN_FRCST</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1775817984</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>Elton.Manoku</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1775818154</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>Elton.Manoku</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>A production forecast estimates the quantities and margin of all fluids produced, injected and imported with energy consumption and emissions levels. The forecast is created annually, extends to end of field life and is incorporated into the Business Plan to generate the annual production targets and booked resources volume (ARPR).

or 

Production Forecast  estimates the quantities of all fluids (liquids and gases) produced, injected and imported feedstocks with energy consumption and associated emissions levels.  Forecasts should be consistent with the booked resources volume. Forecasts could be probabilistic or deterministic. Forecasts provide fundamental input to all Upstream and Integrated Gas Lines of businesses decisions ranging from strategic global aspects to integrated activity and production planning at the Asset level. Forecasts are needed as a prediction of future production and revenues and they are used all the way from Exploration to Abandonment. Forecasts are created monthly, quarterly, and annually. The forecast that is created annually, extends to end of field life and is incorporated into the Business Plan to generate the annual production targets and Annual Review of Petroleum Resources (ARPR). It is important that the forecast is represented on a revenue basis. </a:Comment>
<a:Description>{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\nouicompat\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}
{\*\generator Riched20 10.0.17763}\viewkind4\uc1 
\pard\f0\fs20 Production Forecast\~ estimates the quantities of all fluids (liquids and gases) produced, injected and imported feedstocks with energy consumption and associated emissions levels.  Forecasts should be consistent with the booked resources volume. Forecasts could be probabilistic or deterministic. Forecasts provide fundamental input to all Upstream and Integrated Gas Lines of businesses decisions ranging from strategic global aspects to integrated activity and production planning at the Asset level. Forecasts are needed as a prediction of future production and revenues and they are used all the way from Exploration to Abandonment. Forecasts are created monthly, quarterly, and annually. The forecast that is created annually, extends to end of field life and is incorporated into the Business Plan to generate the annual production targets and Annual Review of Petroleum Resources (ARPR). It is important that the forecast is represented on a revenue basis. \par
}
</a:Description>
