<o:Entity Id="o210">
<a:ObjectID>1644469B-9EA5-4C71-972B-6B7625B0C258</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>Equipment</a:Name>
<a:Code>EQPMNT</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1775817984</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>Elton.Manoku</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1775818154</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>Elton.Manoku</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>A manufactured, non-consumable object that is acquired, assembled, maintained and commissioned as having specific characteristics to deliver the required functionality of zero, one or more FACILITIES over time</a:Comment>
<a:Description>{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\nouicompat\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fswiss\fprq2\fcharset0 Calibri;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}
{\*\generator Riched20 10.0.22000}\viewkind4\uc1 
\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl252\slmult1\ul\f0\fs22 CSI:\ulnone  \par
Equipment itself is not typically considered commercially sensitive information (CSI). Equipment refers to physical assets, machinery, or tools used in various industries and sectors.\par
However, it is important to note that certain details or information related to equipment may be considered commercially sensitive or confidential. This could include proprietary designs, technical specifications, manufacturing processes, pricing information, or any other sensitive information that, if disclosed, could harm the competitive position or business interests of a company.\par
Organizations often have measures in place to protect the confidentiality of sensitive equipment-related information. This can include access controls, non-disclosure agreements, intellectual property protections, and other security protocols to ensure that the confidential information is only accessible to authorized individuals within the organization or trusted partners.\par
While equipment itself may not be considered CSI, it is important to handle and protect any sensitive information associated with equipment to maintain the competitive advantage and safeguard business interests.\par
\ul Confidentiality:\par
\ulnone In general, equipment information is not considered confidential in the same sense as commercially sensitive information. Equipment refers to physical assets, machinery, or tools used in various industries, and the general knowledge of the existence and purpose of equipment is typically not considered confidential.\par
However, certain details or information related to equipment may be considered confidential. This could include proprietary designs, technical specifications, manufacturing processes, pricing information, or any other sensitive information that, if disclosed, could harm the competitive position or business interests of a company.\par
Organizations often have measures in place to protect the confidentiality of sensitive equipment-related information. This can include access controls, non-disclosure agreements, intellectual property protections, and other security protocols to ensure that the confidential information is only accessible to authorized individuals within the organization or trusted partners.\par
While equipment information itself may not be considered confidential, it is important to handle and protect any sensitive information associated with equipment to maintain the competitive advantage and safeguard business interests.\par
\ul Criticality:\par
\ulnone Master data serves as the foundation for various business processes and systems within an organization. It is used for activities such as sales, marketing, supply chain management, financial reporting, and regulatory compliance. Accurate and reliable master data is crucial for effective decision-making, operational efficiency, and maintaining data integrity.\ul\par
Entity Category:\ulnone\par
Master data, as the data refers to the core data entities that are essential for the operations and decision-making processes of an organization.\par
\par

\pard\f1\fs20\par
}
</a:Description>
