<o:Entity Id="o927">
<a:ObjectID>7E031ECE-4ACE-40CB-848A-264E3E5C8514</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>Flow Rates</a:Name>
<a:Code>FLW_RTS</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1775817984</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>Elton.Manoku</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1775818154</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>Elton.Manoku</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>Total reservoir fluid rate represents the total rate of all fluids flowing over a given time interval.  In developing the pressure transient analysis equations, a single-phase flow of fluid is often assumed and in many cases this is valid.  However, there are many cases this single-phase assumption is not valid where oil, gas, and water all flow simultaneously in the reservoir.
</a:Comment>
<a:Description>{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\nouicompat\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Futura Medium;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}
{\colortbl ;\red89\green89\blue89;}
{\*\generator Riched20 10.0.17763}\viewkind4\uc1 
\pard\cf1\kerning24\f0\fs16 Total reservoir fluid rate represents the total rate of all fluids flowing over a given time interval.  In developing the pressure transient analysis equations, a single-phase flow of fluid is often assumed and in many cases this is valid.  However, there are many cases this single-phase assumption is not valid where oil, gas, and water all flow simultaneously in the reservoir.\par

\pard\cf0\kerning0\f1\fs20\par
}
</a:Description>
