<o:Entity Id="o1437">
<a:ObjectID>8ED59011-3E1F-4D90-BDFB-66DAB4DA61AB</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>Counterparty</a:Name>
<a:Code>CNTRPRTY</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1775817984</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>Elton.Manoku</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1775818154</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>Elton.Manoku</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>A counterparty is generally accepted as a party to a contract or the other party to a financial transaction. A counterparty is usually the entity with whom one negotiates on a given agreement, and the term can refer to either party or both, depending on context. Counterparty can be, possibly mutually inclusive, a customer, a supplier, a trader, an operator and a shipper. Counterparty has several properties, such as credit rating, legal/billing addresses, contact details.

The Counterparty Lifecycle Management (CLCM) program defines a T&amp;S counterparty as:
A counterparty is a legal entity in which purchases, or sells are made to” – CLCM definition

Counterparties can be individuals or companies and include third party companies as well as Shell legal entities. Counterparties do not include G&amp;A vendors providing services such as printers and stationery that are managed via the procurement process.
</a:Comment>
<a:ExtendedAttributesText>{C6AC31EB-C90E-4811-BE11-ED1545ECDA32},SHL_CDM_EntityPropertyExtension,1042={0B6B8263-E277-43E7-A4EC-90E8C0E627AB},MTED_EDaM_Entity_Availability_Indication,13=&lt;unspecified&gt;
