<o:Entity Id="o1498">
<a:ObjectID>E0C35723-F0F8-4CE7-9E4E-158E5A282DBF</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>Business Partner</a:Name>
<a:Code>BSNSS_PRTNR</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1775817984</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>Elton.Manoku</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1775818154</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>Elton.Manoku</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>A partner entity of interest to the enterprise, represented as either an Organisation or a Person. It enables the combination of typical roles played by a party into a single object that represents the entity and its information that is common regardless of the role it is playing e.g. a Supplier or a Customer.

It can contain other counter parties Shell is dealing with (like government relations, NGO’s, Organisational Units, as well as other parts of the Shell organisation, i.e. it includes both internal and external organisations or persons that are relevant from Shell&#39;s business point of view.

BUSINESS_PARTNER is the legal entity with whom we do business. This is a common profile for customer, vendor and counterparty that is centrally mastered. Business partners can be related to each other in the form of parent-child relationships or other relationships e.g. groups. 

BUSINESS_PARTNERs are mostly organisations and in rare cases can be persons. To maintain high data quality, every business partner should have a 1:1 link to an ORGANISATION. Shell masters the BUSINESS_PARTNER whereas ORGANISATION is mastered by an external data provider (Dun &amp; Bradstreet).

A business partner is a natural person, organization in which a company has a business interest. A business partner may be a person (Mr. Tom Jones), organization (Electro Products Inc.), or group within a company (Sales dept. of Electro Products Inc).
A business partner can have several roles at one time. Roles are determined by role categories, e.g. ordering party, prospective customers, payer or payee.
A business partner can have relationships with other business partners. Relationships are determined by relationship categories, for example subsidiary of, contact person for, marriage or identical to.
A business partner can have more than one address. Addresses are determined using address types such as delivery address or correspondence address.
Using role determination, several business partners can be allocated to one business partner. Partner functions are used to define the rights and responsibilities of each partner type in a business transaction. A business partner can be defined as a payee for another business partner.


</a:Comment>
<a:Generated>0</a:Generated>
